# Component Development Standards

## Component Architecture

### Component Categories

1. **Base UI Components** (`/components/ui`)
   - Primitive, reusable components
   - Based on Radix UI primitives
   - Examples: `button.tsx`, `dialog.tsx`, `accordion.tsx`
   - Highly composable with minimal business logic

2. **Element Components** (`/components/elements`)
   - Form inputs and controls
   - Examples: `Button`, `TextField`, `ComboboxSelect`
   - Contain controlled/uncontrolled logic

3. **Feature Components** (`/components/[FeatureName]`)
   - Business logic components
   - Examples: `FlightSearchForm`, `FlightCard`, `PassengerSelector`
   - May compose UI and element components

4. **Layout Components** (`/components/layout`)
   - Page structure components
   - Examples: `Header`, `Footer`, `ActiveMenuProvider`

### Component File Structure

For simple components:
```
Button/
  Button.tsx
  Button.types.ts (if types are complex)
```

For complex components:
```
FlightCard/
  FlightCard.tsx
  FlightCardHeader.tsx
  FlightCardDetails.tsx
  index.ts (for clean exports)
  types.ts (shared types)
```

## Component Props Patterns

### Props Interface Naming
- Use `ComponentNameProps` pattern
- Example: `FlightSearchFormProps`, `ButtonProps`

### Props Structure
```typescript
type ComponentProps = {
  // Required props first
  required: string
  data: DataType
  
  // Optional props
  optional?: number
  className?: string
  
  // Event handlers
  onClick?: () => void
  onChange?: (value: string) => void
  
  // Render props / children
  children?: React.ReactNode
  renderHeader?: () => React.ReactNode
}
```

### Default Props
- Use default parameter values in destructuring
- Example:
  ```typescript
  export function Component({ 
    size = "medium",
    variant = "primary",
    disabled = false 
  }: ComponentProps) {
    // ...
  }
  ```

## Component Composition

### Compound Components Pattern
When components work together as a system:
```typescript
export function Card({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <div className="card">{children}</div>
}

Card.Header = function CardHeader({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <div className="card-header">{children}</div>
}

Card.Body = function CardBody({ children }: { children: React.ReactNode }) {
  return <div className="card-body">{children}</div>
}

// Usage:
<Card>
  <Card.Header>Title</Card.Header>
  <Card.Body>Content</Card.Body>
</Card>
```

### Render Props Pattern
For flexible rendering:
```typescript
type ListProps<T> = {
  items: T[]
  renderItem: (item: T) => React.ReactNode
}

export function List<T>({ items, renderItem }: ListProps<T>) {
  return <div>{items.map(renderItem)}</div>
}
```

## Form Components

### Form Component Standards
Reference: [components/FlightSearchForm/FlightSearchForm.tsx](mdc:components/FlightSearchForm/FlightSearchForm.tsx)

1. Use React Hook Form with Controller for controlled inputs
2. Use Zod for validation schemas
3. Implement proper error handling and display
4. Support loading states
5. Provide accessibility attributes

Example:
```typescript
import { Controller, useForm } from "react-hook-form"
import { z } from "zod"
import { zodResolver } from "@hookform/resolvers/zod"

const schema = z.object({
  email: z.string().email({ message: "Invalid email" })
})

type FormValues = z.infer<typeof schema>

export function FormComponent() {
  const { control, handleSubmit } = useForm<FormValues>({
    resolver: zodResolver(schema)
  })
  
  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit(onSubmit)}>
      <Controller
        name="email"
        control={control}
        render={({ field, fieldState }) => (
          <>
            <Input {...field} hasError={!!fieldState.error} />
            {fieldState.error && <Error>{fieldState.error.message}</Error>}
          </>
        )}
      />
    </form>
  )
}
```

## Styling Components

### className Prop Pattern
- Always accept `className` prop for composition
- Use `clsx` or `cn` utility to merge classes
- Example:
  ```typescript
  import { cn } from "@/lib/utils"
  
  type Props = {
    className?: string
  }
  
  export function Component({ className }: Props) {
    return (
      <div className={cn("base-class", "default-styles", className)}>
        Content
      </div>
    )
  }
  ```

### Tailwind Class Organization
Order classes logically:
1. Layout (display, position, flex/grid)
2. Sizing (width, height, padding, margin)
3. Typography (font, text size, color)
4. Visual (background, border, shadow)
5. Interactive (hover, focus, active states)

Example:
```typescript
className="flex items-center justify-between w-full h-12 px-4 py-2 text-sm font-medium text-gray-900 bg-white border border-gray-300 rounded-lg hover:bg-gray-50 focus:outline-none focus:ring-2"
```

### Variant Patterns
Use `class-variance-authority` (cva) for component variants:
```typescript
import { cva, type VariantProps } from "class-variance-authority"

const buttonVariants = cva("base-button-classes", {
  variants: {
    intent: {
      primary: "bg-primary text-white",
      secondary: "bg-gray-200 text-gray-900"
    },
    size: {
      small: "px-3 py-1 text-sm",
      large: "px-6 py-3 text-lg"
    }
  },
  defaultVariants: {
    intent: "primary",
    size: "medium"
  }
})

type ButtonProps = VariantProps<typeof buttonVariants> & {
  // other props
}
```

## Component State Management

### Local State
- Use `useState` for UI state
- Keep state as close to where it's used as possible
- Example:
  ```typescript
  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false)
  const [selectedValue, setSelectedValue] = useState<string | null>(null)
  ```

### Derived State
- Use `useMemo` for computed values
- Example:
  ```typescript
  const filteredItems = useMemo(
    () => items.filter(item => item.active),
    [items]
  )
  ```

### State Initialization
- Use lazy initialization for expensive initial state
- Example:
  ```typescript
  const [data, setData] = useState(() => expensiveComputation())
  ```

## Accessibility

### ARIA Attributes
- Always include proper ARIA labels
- Use semantic HTML when possible
- Example:
  ```typescript
  <button
    aria-label="Close dialog"
    aria-expanded={isOpen}
    aria-controls="dialog-content"
  >
    Close
  </button>
  ```

### Keyboard Navigation
- Ensure all interactive elements are keyboard accessible
- Handle `onKeyDown` for custom interactions
- Support Tab navigation

### Focus Management
- Use `autoFocus` sparingly
- Trap focus in modals/dialogs
- Return focus after closing modals

## Performance Optimization

### Memoization
- Use `React.memo` for components that render often with same props
- Use `useMemo` for expensive calculations
- Use `useCallback` for functions passed to memoized children

Example:
```typescript
export const ExpensiveComponent = React.memo(function ExpensiveComponent({ 
  data 
}: Props) {
  return <div>{/* expensive render */}</div>
})
```

### Lazy Loading
- Use dynamic imports for heavy components
- Example:
  ```typescript
  import dynamic from "next/dynamic"
  
  const HeavyComponent = dynamic(() => import("./HeavyComponent"), {
    loading: () => <Skeleton />,
    ssr: false // if client-only
  })
  ```

## Error Handling

### Error Boundaries
- Wrap risky components in Error Boundaries
- Provide fallback UI
- Log errors for debugging

### Loading States
- Always handle loading states in async components
- Show appropriate loading UI (spinners, skeletons)
- Example:
  ```typescript
  if (isLoading) return <Spinner />
  if (error) return <ErrorMessage error={error} />
  return <Data data={data} />
  ```

## Testing Considerations

- Write components to be easily testable
- Avoid inline complex logic
- Extract business logic to hooks or utilities
- Use data-testid for test targeting when necessary
